OpenGL: clip control keeps clip depth 0..1 where the driver has it

With BGFX_CONFIG_GL_NORMALIZE_NDC_CONVENTIONS every vertex shader maps its 0..1 clip z to OpenGL's -1..1
(gl_Position.z * 2 - w), and OpenGL maps it back to window depth. Through -1..1 a float keeps an absolute
precision: reversed depth, whose values near the far plane are small, loses most of the precision a float depth
buffer gives it on the other backends.

Desktop OpenGL with ARB_clip_control (core in 4.5) now calls glClipControl(GL_LOWER_LEFT, GL_ZERO_TO_ONE) at init
and on each window's context, and the NDC rewrite leaves clip z as it is there. The origin stays lower left: the
rewrite still flips y offscreen. OpenGL ES and WebGL keep the mapping.

